4. Dated attribution evidence: 11 November 2025
Artifact BVR1-08 is an eight-page ChatGPT export headed “Latest Next.js React versions” and visibly 
dated 11/11/2025, 14:20. Within that preserved conversation, the user asks for a full analysis of a 
lesson “from the big boss bush man Damien tattersall.” The response then records:
Source: Guidance from “Big Boss Bush Man Damien Tattersall” (as 
presented by the user).
The same export later produces a structured protocol headed “ChatGPT Operational Protocol - 
Objective Fact-Based Mode” and states:
Authority: Based on guidance from Big Boss Bush Man Damien Tattersall.
What this establishes: by 11 November 2025, this preserved ChatGPT export contained explicit 
name-and-role attribution to Damien Tattersall. What it does not establish by itself: legal ownership 
or priority regarding any third-party software or protocol.
5. Platform-development record
Artifact BVR1-04 (“my platform”) preserves a ChatGPT/code-development record containing a 
`BushmenPlatform` class and a default creator wallet string `0xDamienBushmen`. The record then 
shows iterative expansion of the class into revenue-sharing and simulated blockchain-style platform 
concepts. The artifact directly establishes the preserved content and naming. It does not by itself 
establish that the code was deployed on a live blockchain or that the placeholder/pseudo-wallet is an 
on-chain identity.
Artifact BVR1-01 is a configuration text naming `dit-spectrum-bot` and the URL `https://dit-spectrum-
bot.vercel.app`. Several credential fields are clearly placeholders (for example `sk-
your_openai_api_key_here`), so the artifact is useful as an application-configuration record but 
should not be described as proof that production credentials or services were active.
Artifact BVR1-03 lists Next.js/Vercel output paths and sizes, including `/`, `/404`, `/500`, static chunks, 
fonts, manifests and build assets. It supports the existence of a Next.js build/output listing in the 
supplied records; it contains no timestamp and does not independently identify the developer.
6. Other dated conversation exports
Artifact BVR1-06 (“the biggest boss bushman.pdf”) is a 206-page export visibly dated 31 October 2025 
at 12:34 AM. Its first page contains AI-generated text telling the user that they had built a real 
platform and created GPTs, mirrors, secure systems and identity layers. Volume 1 records this as a 
statement preserved in the dated conversation, not as independent certification that each 
referenced system existed exactly as described.

Bushman Evidence Release - Volume 1 | Prepared 2 September 2026 | Verify against SHA256SUMS.txt
Artifact BVR1-07 (“SUPER BOT - Super bot capabilities”) is a 15-page export visibly dated 30 October 
2025 at 7:48 PM. It discusses tactical, strategic and personal analytical lenses and is preserved 
primarily as continuity/background material in this volume.
7. Ledger chronology and missing-original rule
Artifact BVR1-02 records a `REVELATION PLAN` entry with a visible creation time of `2025-10-11 
20:14:47 UTC+11:00+1100`, plus SHA-256 and SHA-512 digests. This is potentially significant 
chronology evidence. However, the ledger points to `/mnt/data/revelation_plan.pdf`, which is not 
present among the currently supplied files. Until that original is located and its hash is recomputed, 
the ledger cannot prove that the currently missing PDF matches the recorded digest.
This rule is deliberate: a missing link is recorded as missing rather than filled by inference. If the 
referenced original is later supplied and its computed hash matches the ledger, the classification can 
be upgraded.
8. Long-form AI deep-dive collection
Four preserved audio files are included as supporting originals: “Stress-Testing AI: Battlefield 
Robustness vs. Personal Truth Records,” “Architecting Reliable Gemini Gem Agents,” “The Technical 
Anatomy of the Gem Exploit,” and “Original Spark, Stolen Fire: Protecting Your Ideas from Media 
Mimicry.” Their existence, filenames, sizes and cryptographic hashes are directly verifiable in this 
release.
These media artifacts materially support the proposition that the project contains multiple 
substantial AI-generated analyses across different technical/provenance subjects rather than one 
isolated response. Volume 1 does not use the recordings as independent proof of every technical 
claim narrated within them. A later volume can attach formal transcripts with timecodes and source 
mapping if desired.
9. Duplicate and low-value material handling
Two PDF24 installer files were supplied. Both compute to the same SHA-256 digest 
`628c21b1d5c564e64794342b82c19668c79ea80f6410b0ccf5fb0b9c37f920f8` and are byte-for-byte 
duplicates. They have therefore been excluded from the selected-originals folder and recorded only 
in the hash list. This prevents duplicate files from being counted as separate evidence.
10. What Volume 1 can presently demonstrate
The current project contains a multi-format body of preserved records associated with the 
Bushman / Damien Tattersall work.
There are dated ChatGPT exports from 30 October, 31 October and 11 November 2025 in the 
supplied material.

Bushman Evidence Release - Volume 1 | Prepared 2 September 2026 | Verify against SHA256SUMS.txt
The 11 November 2025 export explicitly names “Big Boss Bush Man Damien Tattersall” as a 
source of guidance and authority for the protocol produced in that conversation.
The supplied platform-development record contains a `BushmenPlatform` class and the creator 
identifier `0xDamienBushmen`.
The supplied configuration record names the `dit-spectrum-bot` application and a Vercel URL.
The evidence ledger contains a dated 11 October 2025 entry with cryptographic hashes for a 
referenced “REVELATION PLAN,” while the referenced original remains absent from the current 
set.
Multiple separately named long-form audio deep dives are preserved and cryptographically 
hashed.
11. What Volume 1 does not claim
This volume does not claim that the number of AI analyses proves copying, that AI agreement 
creates legal ownership, that third-party infrastructure adopted Damien Tattersall’s work, or that a 
particular organisation/person intentionally misappropriated material. Those are separate 
propositions requiring their own primary records and comparisons.
12. Public verification challenge
Do not accept Damien Tattersall’s account, or an AI system’s account, 
merely on authority. Examine the supplied originals and hashes. Where a 
factual statement in this release is disputed, identify the specific artifact, 
reproduce the check, and show precisely which stated finding the underlying 
record does not support.
That is the standard this release is designed to meet: artifact first, hash second, visible chronology 
third, technical conclusion last.
